{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Oregon's drug decriminalization program, which began as the first in the nation, ends on Sunday. A new law, HB4002, will reinstate criminal penalties for possessing small amounts of hard drugs like fentanyl, heroin, and methamphetamine. Individuals caught with these substances will be offered a choice: either face possession charges or participate in a treatment program. This program involves completing a behavioral health program and participating in a \"deflection program\" designed to avoid fines.\n\nThe potential punishment for personal-use possession is up to six months in jail. The law intends to help police address public drug use and increase penalties for selling drugs near locations like parks. Counties are encouraged, but not required, to establish treatment options to redirect individuals from the legal system to addiction and mental health services. This allows local governments to tailor programs to their specific resources.\n\nLawmakers supporting the law argue that it enables counties to create programs aligned with their capabilities, while critics express concerns about a potentially inconsistent and unfair approach to policy. Portland police chief Bob Day explained the deflection process: officers will contact the county's deflection dispatch line if someone qualifies, and a mobile behavioral health team will attempt to connect within 30 minutes. If this isn’t possible, jail time will be imposed.\n\nThis shift in policy aims to address drug use more directly while providing a pathway to treatment. It’s a significant change in approach to substance use, impacting the lives of individuals and potentially reshaping public safety. The law's effects will be closely watched as a model for other regions in the country exploring alternative approaches to drug policy.\n\nP.P.S. The complexities of this situation, involving various factors like age and access to resources, highlight the need for nuanced solutions. People may need to eat and shop for necessities, and understanding the nuances of a different language can be essential to effective communication.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.0010344398906454444}, {"token": "'", "logprob": -0.25791406631469727}, {"token": "s", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁drug", "logprob": -0.056506264954805374}, {"token": "▁decriminal", "logprob": -3.6477376852417365e-05}, {"token": "ization", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁program", "logprob": -0.025647783651947975}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.3391120135784149}, {"token": "▁which", "logprob": -0.4383833408355713}, {"token": "▁began", "logprob": -0.06941577047109604}, {"token": "▁as", "logprob": -0.009918688796460629}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.3956194818019867}, {"token": "▁first", "logprob": -0.2586032748222351}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -0.07627439498901367}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.0006243425305001438}, {"token": "▁nation", "logprob": -0.8511945009231567}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0005638201837427914}, {"token": "▁ends", "logprob": -0.8672029972076416}, {"token": "▁on", "logprob": -0.5512009859085083}, {"token": "▁Sunday", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.04176599159836769}, {"token": "▁A", "logprob": -0.02025071159005165}, {"token": "▁new", "logprob": -0.008278105407953262}, {"token": "▁law", "logprob": -0.0032522189430892467}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0016035091830417514}, {"token": "▁HB", "logprob": -0.00010406429646536708}, {"token": "4", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.3589766240329482e-05}, {"token": "▁will", "logprob": -0.28507688641548157}, {"token": "▁reinstate", "logprob": -0.09146003425121307}, {"token": "▁criminal", "logprob": -0.0032870331779122